// Compiled by bin/build-help.php from help/*.md (the add-on's topics only). Do not edit.
export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"trusscraft","name":"TrussCraft","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"trusscraft-start","scope":"addon:trusscraft","title":"TrussCraft: getting started","summary":"Design timber roof trusses in BimCraft, PartCraft, ModelCraft and CadCraft: common trusses, hip sets, girders with jacks and valley sets, your own loads and plate tables, the member, cut and joint lists and a first member check. A design aid, not engineered truss design.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["truss","trusses","roof truss","timber","hip","valley","girder","jack","king post","queen post","howe","pratt","fink","scissor","mono","attic","cut list","method of joints","trusscraft","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"not-a-substitute-for-a-truss-designer","text":"Not a substitute for a truss designer","level":2},{"id":"what-is-yours-to-set","text":"What is yours to set","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitch the add-on on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ETrusses\u003C/strong\u003E tab. Choose the type, the span (bearing to bearing), the pitch, the overhang, the heel height and the spacing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoads and combinations (yours)\u003C/span\u003E type the pressures and the combination factors of the standard you work to. They start at zero or blank: TrussCraft carries no code table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesign one truss\u003C/span\u003E. TrussCraft makes the members, finds the forces by the method of joints for each of your combinations, chooses the smallest sawn sizes that pass, and draws the elevation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the roof: BimCraft reads the roofs of the model; in PartCraft, ModelCraft and CadCraft type the outline&#039;s corners or take them from what you selected (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake outline\u003C/span\u003E button), and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELay out on the roof\u003C/span\u003E. The roof&#039;s own geometry decides the trusses: commons on the ridge stretch, and at a hip end either step-down trusses or a girder that carries jack and corner trusses. The table under the buttons lists every distinct truss; click a row to draw it. The 3D view turns with the two sliders.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe place button puts the set in your app in one undo step; what it makes depends on the app (see the topic for your app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrussCraft in your app\u003C/span\u003E). The buttons under it save the member list, the cut list for the whole set, the joint schedule, a stock length plan, a life-size jig drawing of one truss, and the set as STL or OBJ.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-a-substitute-for-a-truss-designer\"\u003ENot a substitute for a truss designer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is a design aid. It is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot a substitute for engineered truss design or manufacturer sign-off\u003C/strong\u003E, and the Problems panel says so every time, first. The check is axial force by the method of joints on loads at the panel points (plus chord bending between panel points when you give an Fb). It leaves out joint slip, plate and tooth capacity, bracing design, unbalanced snow and drift. Bearings, uplift and a bracing checklist appear as Problems for the truss designer to design. A truss TrussCraft cannot make is named in Problems; nothing is dropped quietly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-yours-to-set\"\u003EWhat is yours to set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe species and grade table, the sizes and the dead-load starting values are labelled typical values, rounded, and not a standard&#039;s table. Wind, uplift, combination factors, Fb, bearing stress and the deflection limit start empty. Load your own species table and your own connector plate table (CSV or JSON) to replace the typical values and the placeholder plates.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons. Open the Trusses tab. Choose the type, the span (bearing to bearing), the pitch, the overhang, the heel height and the spacing. Under Loads and combinations (yours) type the pressures and the combination factors of the standard you work to. They start at zero or blank: TrussCraft carries no code table. Click Design one truss. TrussCraft makes the members, finds the forces by the method of joints for each of your combinations, chooses the smallest sawn sizes that pass, and draws the elevation. Give the roof: BimCraft reads the roofs of the model; in PartCraft, ModelCraft and CadCraft type the outline's corners or take them from what you selected (the Take outline button), and click Lay out on the roof. The roof's own geometry decides the trusses: commons on the ridge stretch, and at a hip end either step-down trusses or a girder that carries jack and corner trusses. The table under the buttons lists every distinct truss; click a row to draw it. The 3D view turns with the two sliders. The place button puts the set in your app in one undo step; what it makes depends on the app (see the topic for your app, TrussCraft in your app). The buttons under it save the member list, the cut list for the whole set, the joint schedule, a stock length plan, a life-size jig drawing of one truss, and the set as STL or OBJ. Not a substitute for a truss designer This is a design aid. It is not a substitute for engineered truss design or manufacturer sign-off, and the Problems panel says so every time, first. The check is axial force by the method of joints on loads at the panel points (plus chord bending between panel points when you give an Fb). It leaves out joint slip, plate and tooth capacity, bracing design, unbalanced snow and drift. Bearings, uplift and a bracing checklist appear as Problems for the truss designer to design. A truss TrussCraft cannot make is named in Problems; nothing is dropped quietly. What is yours to set The species and grade table, the sizes and the dead-load starting values are labelled typical values, rounded, and not a standard's table. Wind, uplift, combination factors, Fb, bearing stress and the deflection limit start empty. Load your own species table and your own connector plate table (CSV or JSON) to replace the typical values and the placeholder plates."},{"slug":"trusscraft-roofs","scope":"addon:trusscraft","title":"TrussCraft: hips, valleys, girders and jacks","summary":"How the roof's own geometry becomes trusses: step-down trusses or a girder carrying jack and corner trusses at a hip end, valley sets over a main roof, and what is reported when something cannot be made.","section":"tasks","order":3,"keywords":["hip","valley","girder","jack truss","corner truss","step-down","valley set","flush framing","wing","mouth","minimum height"],"headings":[{"id":"where-the-trusses-come-from","text":"Where the trusses come from","level":2},{"id":"hip-ends","text":"Hip ends","level":2},{"id":"valleys","text":"Valleys","level":2},{"id":"when-something-cannot-be-made","text":"When something cannot be made","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-trusses-come-from\"\u003EWhere the trusses come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrussCraft reads the roof BimCraft drew (or one made from the corners you type or pick in any of the four apps: the same straight-skeleton roof geometry BimCraft uses, at one pitch) and cuts it with the plane each truss stands in. The cut is the truss&#039;s top chord, so a common truss is a triangle, a hip end shows a flat top, and a section across a valley has the valley as a joint. Every truss is a pin-jointed frame TrussCraft proves stable before it uses it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hip-ends\"\u003EHip ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStep-down trusses\u003C/strong\u003E (default). Each station in the hip end is a full-span truss with a flat top at the height the hip plane has there (distance from the end wall times the tangent of the pitch), level between the hip lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGirder carrying jack and corner trusses.\u003C/strong\u003E The girder is the flat-topped truss at the setback you give (0 = half the hip). Between it and the end wall, jack trusses run from the wall to the girder across the middle, and corner trusses near the side walls rise to the hip line and then run level. Jacks end flush at the girder&#039;s top. Each jack&#039;s high-end reaction is applied to the girder as a point load, case by case, so the girder is designed with them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStations lower than \u003Cstrong\u003ESmallest truss made\u003C/strong\u003E (300 mm unless you change it) are \u003Cstrong\u003Enot made\u003C/strong\u003E. Problems says how many, and where, so the hip end there is framed by hand on the wall plate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"valleys\"\u003EValleys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrusses span across the valley.\u003C/strong\u003E A station that crosses a valley is one truss with the valley as a kink in its top chord. Problems notes each one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EValley set over the main roof.\u003C/strong\u003E For a wing roof that meets a main roof square to its ridge (a T), the main trusses stop at the wing&#039;s mouth, the wing gets its own trusses, and triangular valley trusses stand on the main roof: each spans between the valley lines at its height, with its apex on the wing ridge. You can also type the wing width and both pitches to get the set alone. TrussCraft does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E make the girder or wall along the wing&#039;s mouth and does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E add the valley trusses&#039; reactions to the main trusses; Problems says both.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-something-cannot-be-made\"\u003EWhen something cannot be made\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA truss that cannot be generated (too low, no usable section, a design that fails) is an error in Problems with its name and reason. It is not in the cut list or the model. The truss designer provides it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Where the trusses come from TrussCraft reads the roof BimCraft drew (or one made from the corners you type or pick in any of the four apps: the same straight-skeleton roof geometry BimCraft uses, at one pitch) and cuts it with the plane each truss stands in. The cut is the truss's top chord, so a common truss is a triangle, a hip end shows a flat top, and a section across a valley has the valley as a joint. Every truss is a pin-jointed frame TrussCraft proves stable before it uses it. Hip ends Step-down trusses (default). Each station in the hip end is a full-span truss with a flat top at the height the hip plane has there (distance from the end wall times the tangent of the pitch), level between the hip lines. Girder carrying jack and corner trusses. The girder is the flat-topped truss at the setback you give (0 = half the hip). Between it and the end wall, jack trusses run from the wall to the girder across the middle, and corner trusses near the side walls rise to the hip line and then run level. Jacks end flush at the girder's top. Each jack's high-end reaction is applied to the girder as a point load, case by case, so the girder is designed with them. Stations lower than Smallest truss made (300 mm unless you change it) are not made. Problems says how many, and where, so the hip end there is framed by hand on the wall plate. Valleys Trusses span across the valley. A station that crosses a valley is one truss with the valley as a kink in its top chord. Problems notes each one. Valley set over the main roof. For a wing roof that meets a main roof square to its ridge (a T), the main trusses stop at the wing's mouth, the wing gets its own trusses, and triangular valley trusses stand on the main roof: each spans between the valley lines at its height, with its apex on the wing ridge. You can also type the wing width and both pitches to get the set alone. TrussCraft does not make the girder or wall along the wing's mouth and does not add the valley trusses' reactions to the main trusses; Problems says both. When something cannot be made A truss that cannot be generated (too low, no usable section, a design that fails) is an error in Problems with its name and reason. It is not in the cut list or the model. The truss designer provides it."},{"slug":"trusscraft-bimcraft","scope":"addon:trusscraft","title":"TrussCraft in BimCraft","summary":"What TrussCraft does in BimCraft: roofs read from the model, every timber piece placed as an IfcMember.","section":"tasks","order":5,"keywords":["truss","trusses","trusscraft","bimcraft","ifc","ifcmember","roof","place"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-get","text":"What you get","level":2},{"id":"what-it-cannot-do","text":"What it cannot do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-get\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe roofs of the open model are listed under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout on a roof\u003C/span\u003E; TrussCraft uses the roof&#039;s own pitch, overhang, gables and wall-top height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/span\u003E writes each timber piece as an IfcMember (CHORD, STRUT or POST in IFC4) with its section, species and truss mark, in one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-cannot-do\"\u003EWhat it cannot do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHips, valleys and girders are made as the roof&#039;s geometry needs them. Problems lists what is not made. The result is not a substitute for engineered truss design.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you get The roofs of the open model are listed under Layout on a roof; TrussCraft uses the roof's own pitch, overhang, gables and wall-top height. Place in the model writes each timber piece as an IfcMember (CHORD, STRUT or POST in IFC4) with its section, species and truss mark, in one undo step. What it cannot do Hips, valleys and girders are made as the roof's geometry needs them. Problems lists what is not made. The result is not a substitute for engineered truss design."},{"slug":"trusscraft-types","scope":"addon:trusscraft","title":"TrussCraft: truss types and what they do","summary":"King post, queen post, Howe, Pratt, Fink, scissor, mono and attic trusses, and the profile trusses a roof's hip and valley make; what TrussCraft can and cannot check.","section":"reference","order":2,"keywords":["king post","queen post","howe","pratt","fink","scissor","mono","attic","truss types","pitch","span","heel","overhang","raised heel","stub"],"headings":[{"id":"the-types","text":"The types","level":2},{"id":"dimensions","text":"Dimensions","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-types\"\u003EThe types\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKing post\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne post at the centre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShort spans.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQueen post\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo posts, a strut between them and a brace\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle panel is a rectangle, so the brace is part of it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHowe\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVerticals and diagonals that rise toward the centre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet the number of panels (even, 4 to 12).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPratt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVerticals and diagonals that fall toward the centre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same panels.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFink\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe W: two webs a side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe common domestic truss.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScissor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBottom chords that rise at a lower pitch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe bearings spread under load; the wall must allow for it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMono\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA single slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPanels as above; the tail is at the low end.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAttic\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA room in the roof\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith pinned corners the room is a mechanism. TrussCraft treats the room&#039;s four corners as rigid (a stated idealisation you can switch off) and solves the room as a frame; the forces depend on that statement.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpan\u003C/strong\u003E is bearing to bearing, outer face to outer face. \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E is the top chord&#039;s slope. \u003Cstrong\u003EOverhang\u003C/strong\u003E is the plan distance the top chord runs past the bearing. \u003Cstrong\u003EHeel height\u003C/strong\u003E is the vertical depth at the outer face. When it is taller than the chords need by 60 mm or more, the truss gets a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebearing stub\u003C/strong\u003E under each heel joint: a real member (ST1, ST2) that carries the vertical reaction in compression and appears in the member list, cut list and placed geometry. The horizontal heel reaction is shear at the stub and its anchorage, which Problems states. A smaller raise is drawn as a heel block. The cut list measures long point to long point along the centre-line, with webs shortened to the chord face.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The types Type Shape Notes King post One post at the centre Short spans. Queen post Two posts, a strut between them and a brace The middle panel is a rectangle, so the brace is part of it. Howe Verticals and diagonals that rise toward the centre Set the number of panels (even, 4 to 12). Pratt Verticals and diagonals that fall toward the centre The same panels. Fink The W: two webs a side The common domestic truss. Scissor Bottom chords that rise at a lower pitch The bearings spread under load; the wall must allow for it. Mono A single slope Panels as above; the tail is at the low end. Attic A room in the roof With pinned corners the room is a mechanism. TrussCraft treats the room's four corners as rigid (a stated idealisation you can switch off) and solves the room as a frame; the forces depend on that statement. Dimensions Span is bearing to bearing, outer face to outer face. Pitch is the top chord's slope. Overhang is the plan distance the top chord runs past the bearing. Heel height is the vertical depth at the outer face. When it is taller than the chords need by 60 mm or more, the truss gets a bearing stub under each heel joint: a real member (ST1, ST2) that carries the vertical reaction in compression and appears in the member list, cut list and placed geometry. The horizontal heel reaction is shear at the stub and its anchorage, which Problems states. A smaller raise is drawn as a heel block. The cut list measures long point to long point along the centre-line, with webs shortened to the chord face."},{"slug":"trusscraft-loads","scope":"addon:trusscraft","title":"TrussCraft: loads, combinations and tables","summary":"Enter your own dead, snow or live, wind and uplift pressures and combination factors; chord bending, deflection, bearings and uplift; your own species and connector plate tables.","section":"reference","order":4,"keywords":["loads","combinations","wind","uplift","snow","dead","factors","bending","Fb","deflection","bearing","plate table","species table","csv","json","psf","kPa"],"headings":[{"id":"your-loads","text":"Your loads","level":2},{"id":"checks","text":"Checks","level":2},{"id":"your-own-tables","text":"Your own tables","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-loads\"\u003EYour loads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressures are in kPa or psf (choose the unit). TrussCraft does not know any standard&#039;s loads, wind map or snow map: type what your standard gives.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECase\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoof dead load on the slope, ceiling dead load on the bottom chord.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnow or live\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnow (or roof live) on plan; the attic floor live load goes on the room floor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWind\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA pressure normal to the roof, windward on the left half of the span and leeward on the right (+ pushes onto the roof, − is suction).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUplift\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA net upward pressure on plan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach \u003Cstrong\u003Ecombination\u003C/strong\u003E row multiplies the cases by factors you type, with its own duration factor. A row with every factor zero is not run. If wind or uplift pressures are set but no combination uses them, Problems says nothing was run. Under uplift the forces reverse, so a bottom chord that is in tension under gravity is checked as a compression member; each member shows its largest tension, largest compression and governing combination in the member list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"checks\"\u003EChecks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxial\u003C/strong\u003E force by the method of joints, tension on the gross section, compression with a column stability factor from E_min.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBending\u003C/strong\u003E of a chord between panel points: a simple span (w L² / the divisor you set, 8 by default). It is \u003Cstrong\u003Echecked only when you give an Fb\u003C/strong\u003E (or load a species table with one). Without it the bending stress is reported and the Problems say it was not checked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDeflection\u003C/strong\u003E under dead plus snow, by virtual work from the species table&#039;s E. A limit you enter (span / n) is checked; without one a note says none is. Chord bending, joint slip and creep are not in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBearings\u003C/strong\u003E: the largest reaction at a heel, the bearing length it needs when you give an Fc⊥, any net uplift, and the horizontal reaction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBracing\u003C/strong\u003E checklist: a note for the designer, plus a warning for slender webs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-own-tables\"\u003EYour own tables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELoad a \u003Cstrong\u003Especies table\u003C/strong\u003E (CSV or JSON: id, label, Ft, Fc, E, Emin and optionally Fb and Fcp, in MPa) to replace the typical values. Load a \u003Cstrong\u003Econnector plate table\u003C/strong\u003E (CSV or JSON: id, w, h, kN, optionally a label line \u003Ccode\u003E# label: name\u003C/code\u003E) and the joint schedule uses it, marked as yours; until then every plate is labelled &quot;Placeholder, not a manufacturer value&quot;. TrussCraft never invents a capacity: a joint larger than the biggest plate in the table says so.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your loads Pressures are in kPa or psf (choose the unit). TrussCraft does not know any standard's loads, wind map or snow map: type what your standard gives. Case Meaning Dead Roof dead load on the slope, ceiling dead load on the bottom chord. Snow or live Snow (or roof live) on plan; the attic floor live load goes on the room floor. Wind A pressure normal to the roof, windward on the left half of the span and leeward on the right (+ pushes onto the roof, − is suction). Uplift A net upward pressure on plan. Each combination row multiplies the cases by factors you type, with its own duration factor. A row with every factor zero is not run. If wind or uplift pressures are set but no combination uses them, Problems says nothing was run. Under uplift the forces reverse, so a bottom chord that is in tension under gravity is checked as a compression member; each member shows its largest tension, largest compression and governing combination in the member list. Checks Axial force by the method of joints, tension on the gross section, compression with a column stability factor from E_min. Bending of a chord between panel points: a simple span (w L² / the divisor you set, 8 by default). It is checked only when you give an Fb (or load a species table with one). Without it the bending stress is reported and the Problems say it was not checked. Deflection under dead plus snow, by virtual work from the species table's E. A limit you enter (span / n) is checked; without one a note says none is. Chord bending, joint slip and creep are not in it. Bearings: the largest reaction at a heel, the bearing length it needs when you give an Fc⊥, any net uplift, and the horizontal reaction. Bracing checklist: a note for the designer, plus a warning for slender webs. Your own tables Load a species table (CSV or JSON: id, label, Ft, Fc, E, Emin and optionally Fb and Fcp, in MPa) to replace the typical values. Load a connector plate table (CSV or JSON: id, w, h, kN, optionally a label line # label: name) and the joint schedule uses it, marked as yours; until then every plate is labelled \"Placeholder, not a manufacturer value\". TrussCraft never invents a capacity: a joint larger than the biggest plate in the table says so."}]};
